


Here are the main objectives of subnetting in short:

1. Efficient IP Address Utilization Preventing Address Waste: 
Under older classful addressing rules, 
a company needing more than 254 hosts was forced to take an entire Class B network (which allocates up to 65,534 hosts), 
wasting massive amounts of global address space.   

Custom Sizing: Subnetting allows network administrators to carve out custom-sized address ranges (using Variable Length Subnet Masking or CIDR) 
that closely match the exact needs of specific networks or departments without wasting IP addresses.   

2. Reduced Network Traffic and Improved PerformanceMinimizing Broadcast Domains: Devices on a local network broadcast messages (like ARP requests) to all other devices. 
If a network is massive, this broadcast traffic can bog down performance. 
Subnetting breaks a large network into smaller segments, containing broadcast traffic within its own subnet.
 
 Improve Performance: Reduce network congestion by containing broadcast traffic within smaller segments.
 Enhance Security: Isolate sensitive departments or devices (like guest Wi-Fi vs. finance) using firewalls and access controls.
 Easier Troubleshooting: Isolate network issues quickly within smaller, manageable segments without affecting the whole enterprise.
 Manage Physical Locations: Easily split a single IP range across multiple offices, floors, or departments.



 ========================

 Here is a breakdown of Class A, Class B, and Class C IPv4 addresses, including their total ranges, default subnet masks, and usable IP counts per network:

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| IP Class | First Octet Range             | Default Subnet Mask (CIDR) | Network / Host Split             | Usable Host IPs Per Network |
| -------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------| -------------------------------- | ----------------------------|
| Class A  | 1.0.0.0 to 126.255.255.255    | 255.0.0.0 (`/8`)           | 1 Octet Network / 3 Octets Host  | 16,777,214 per network      |
| Class B  | 128.0.0.0 to 191.255.255.255  | 255.255.0.0 (`/16`)        | 2 Octets Network / 2 Octets Host | 65,534 per network          |
| Class C  | 192.0.0.0 to 223.255.255.255  | 255.255.255.0 (`/24`)      | 3 Octets Network / 1 Octet Host  | 254 per network             |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Note: 127.x.x.x is reserved for loopback 
---

### Usable  Private Range according to  RFC-1918 Standard:

 Class A: Designed for massive networks (like large multinational corporations or ISPs).
 Private Range: `10.0.0.0` to `10.255.255.255` (`/8`)


 Class B: Designed for medium-to-large networks (like universities or mid-sized enterprises).
 Private Range: `172.16.0.0` to `172.31.255.255` (`/12`)


 Class C: Designed for small local area networks (LANs) or home/small business environments.
 Private Range: `192.168.0.0` to `192.168.255.255` (`/16`)







